5parrow  <mail.gatech@gtg692n.edu> wrote:
>David Damerell wrote:
>>Doom runs OK on 486s, but is a bit of a dog on most 386s. However, it is
>>effectively a 2D game for visibility purposes, and uses pregenerated BSP
>>trees to speed things up. This would not be appropriate for a game with
>>randomly generated levels.
>I don't know; it's certainly possible to generate the BSP tree on the 
>fly just before entering the level.

Evidently; that is why I say that it is not appropriate, not impossible.
But if you edited Doom back in the day, you'll remember how BSP builds
were on minimum-spec machines for the game itself.

Also, BSP trees would not work well with a game supporting a diggable
environment, as most roguelikes do.

However, if space is divided into large chunks of known size (as with the
tile layout in 2D roguelikes), pregenerated vision tables are quite
practical.
